course: dictionary
lesson: 10911
title: Vivid words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Vivid describes something that is very bright, very clear, or full of life. «The sky was a vivid red at sunset.» Red that strong is easy to picture, so vivid tells you how bright it is.
explain: The word also works for memories and imaginations. «I can still vividly remember my first day at school.» Vividly is the adverb: it tells you how you remember. It means so clearly that the memory seems real. Ask yourself: is the picture bright, clear and detailed? If so, vivid fits.
explain: Good writers use vivid words so that readers can see, hear and feel the story. Clues such as colours, pictures, dreams and memories tell you which meaning is being used.
fact: Vivid means very bright and strong in colour, as in «vivid colours».
fact: Vivid also means very clear and detailed, as in «a vivid dream» or «a vivid memory».
fact: Vivid can mean lively and full of ideas, as in «a vivid imagination».
fact: The adverb is vividly, as in «I vividly remember the day we won».
fact: The noun is vividness, which means how bright or clear something is.
exercise: «The painting used vivid colours of orange and purple.» What does vivid mean here? | Dull and faded | Very small | Very quiet | *Very bright and strong
exercise: «She had a vivid imagination and wrote stories about dragons.» Which meaning fits? | Very slow | Very hungry | Very cold | *Full of lively ideas
exercise: «Sam vividly remembered the sound of the waves.» What does vividly tell us? | Without any feeling | Sleepily | Quickly and carelessly | *So clearly it seems real
test: «The teacher asked for a vivid description of the castle.» What is a vivid description? | A short whisper | A dull list of facts | A sad story | *A clear, detailed picture in words
test: Which phrase means someone has lots of lively ideas? | A dull colour | A quiet room | A fast car | *A vivid imagination
exam: Which word is the noun from vivid? | vivid | vividly | vivider | *vividness
